2026-09-23 00:01:27,549 | INFO | [congress-pelosi-v4] [agents] name=bear mode=backtesting model=gemini-3.5-flash-lite cache_hit=False tokens_in=21239 tokens_out=453 tokens_cached_in=16179 tokens_uncached_in=5060 tokens_thinking=0 tokens_total=21692 latency_ms=2452 first_event_latency_ms=2451 tool_calls=0 observability_warnings=1 summary='### Comprehensive Bear Case Against Copying the Visible Book (As of 2026-01-26)  1. **Severe Filing Staleness & Point-In-Time Violations**:    Per the user context and research evidence, congressional Periodic Transaction Reports (PTRs) are subject to significant reporting lags. While filing **20033725** carries a report date of January 23, 2026 (just prior to our simulation date of January 26, 2026), filings **20034836** and **20035143** carry report dates strictly in the future relative to our simulation clock. Attempting to incorporate or trade on delayed public disclosures forces the portfolio to react to stale information where prices have already shifted.  2. **Broad Disclosure Ranges & Arbitrary Sizing**:    Congressional filings disclose only broad financial brackets (e.g., $15,001–$50,000, $1M–$5M) rather than precise share quantities, execution prices, or cost bases. Scaling midpoint estimates (such as net allocations across NVDA, TEM, VST, GOOGL, AMZN, PYPL, DIS, and AB) onto a fixed portfolio value introduces massive sizing inaccuracies and speculative exposure that do not reflect true risk parameters.  3. **Execution Friction, Slippage, and Market Impact**:    Replicating high-profile congressional disclosures in retail or institutional sizing creates clustered retail flow. Market participants actively monitor and front-run these disclosures, resulting in wide bid-ask spreads, unfavorable execution prices, and immediate negative slippage upon entry.  4. **Lack of Ongoing Position Transparency & Management**:    Disclosures provide a static snapshot of past transactions without ongoing visibility into subsequent hedging, option overlays, or exit strategies. Blindly mimicking entries without knowing when or how positions are being trimmed or closed leaves the portfolio exposed to unmanaged drawdowns.  RESULT: Argued the comprehensive bear case against copying the visible book, detailing filing staleness, range ambiguities, execution friction, and lack of ongoing management transparency, with no orders submitted.' trace=/Users/robertgrzesik/Library/Caches/lumibot/1.0/agent_runtime/traces/bear/d55b1a8a35079353c964fb7346abb1c8fb78343d8c7ba43e2b7773b7abb6514c-20260923040127523137.json
